<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
        Legacy Xposed scope array (item 1).

        Nuke declares its scope this way and its scope is editable in LSPosed, which is what
        lets the user add an app clone. The same array is provided here so our module behaves
        identically: these ids are the DEFAULT, and the user may add their clone.
    -->
    <string-array name="xposed_scope">
        <item>com.tencent.mm</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.u1</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.u2</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.u3</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.u4</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.u5</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.u6</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.u7</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.u8</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.u9</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.clone</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.dual</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.multiapp</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.parallel</item>
        <item>com.tencent.mm.virtual</item>

        <!--
            v5.0.0: Douyin is the second host, so its packages are declared here too.

            com.ss.android.ugc.aweme is the one the user runs and the only one verified on a device;
            .lite is the lite edition and com.ss.android.ugc.trill the overseas build. The same rule as
            the WeChat clones above applies: these are the DEFAULT, and the user may add their own
            clone in the LSPosed scope page.

            Kept in sync with HostApp.DOUYIN_PACKAGES, which decides the host at runtime.
        -->
        <item>com.ss.android.ugc.aweme</item>
        <item>com.ss.android.ugc.aweme.lite</item>
        <item>com.ss.android.ugc.trill</item>

        <!--
            v6.0.0: Soul is the third host.

            cn.soulapp.android is the ONLY package Soul ships; its binary AndroidManifest.xml was
            parsed directly and reports package="cn.soulapp.android" versionName="6.37.0"
            versionCode="26091510". Unlike WeChat there is no family of clone packages to list; a
            third-party cloner keeps that name (or appends a suffix), and the suffix case is already
            accepted at runtime by the `cn.soulapp.android.` prefix rule in HostApp.of.

            Kept in sync with HostApp.SOUL_PACKAGES, which decides the host at runtime.
        -->
        <item>cn.soulapp.android</item>

        <!--
            v7.0.0: Kuaishou is the fourth host.

            com.smile.gifmaker is the ONLY package Kuaishou ships; its binary AndroidManifest.xml was
            parsed directly and reports package="com.smile.gifmaker" versionName="14.8.30.50423"
            versionCode="50423". Unlike WeChat there is no family of clone packages to list; a
            third-party cloner keeps that name (or appends a suffix), and the suffix case is already
            accepted at runtime by the com.smile.gifmaker. prefix rule in HostApp.of.

            Kept in sync with HostApp.KUAISHOU_PACKAGES, which decides the host at runtime.
        -->
        <item>com.smile.gifmaker</item>

        <!--
            v8.0.0: QQ is the fifth host.

            com.tencent.mobileqq is the ONLY package QQ ships; its binary AndroidManifest.xml was
            parsed directly by _tools/qq/qq_manifest.py and reports
            package=com.tencent.mobileqq versionName=9.3.65 versionCode=16240. Confirmed on the
            device as well: adb shell pm list packages shows exactly one QQ package and NO
            tim / minihd / qqi / HD / lite variant, so unlike WeChat there is no clone-package family
            to list. A third-party cloner that keeps that name (or appends a suffix) is already
            accepted at runtime by the com.tencent.mobileqq. prefix rule in HostApp.of.

            The prefix is the FULL package name plus a dot, never just com.tencent.: that device also
            has com.tencent.qqmusic and com.tencent.qqlive installed, and a looser rule would capture
            them and install QQ hooks into an unrelated app.

            NOTE: io.github.qqglassnav.replica is a THIRD-PARTY app whose name merely mentions QQ. It
            must never be added here.

            QQ's own sub-PROCESSES (com.tencent.mobileqq:video, com.tencent.mobileqq:MSF) are
            deliberately NOT listed: those are process names, not packages, and this array feeds the
            LSPosed scope list, which takes packages. LSPosed reports a ":suffixed" process against
            packageName=com.tencent.mobileqq, which this entry already covers.

            Kept in sync with HostApp.QQ_PACKAGES, which decides the host at runtime.
        -->
        <item>com.tencent.mobileqq</item>

        <!--
            v8.1.0: TIM (腾讯征程 / TIM) joins the QQ host.

            It is a SEPARATE APK with a separate package, but it runs the SAME NT chat engine, so it is
            served by the same hooks. Measured, not assumed: the TIM 4.1.0 APK (154 MB, 24 dex) was
            unpacked and scanned, and every class this module hooks on QQ is present under the same
            com.tencent.qqnt.* and com.tencent.mobileqq.* names: the long-press menu container and its
            no-icon twin, the menu item type, BubblePopupWindow, AIOMsgItem, MsgRecord, AbsMsgListVB,
            the input box and the multi-select event.

            Why it is NOT a sixth host: the module gives each host a package family, a display name and
            a hook set. TIM shares QQ's hook SET, differing only in brand text, so a sixth constant
            would duplicate every host branch in the codebase.

            The com.tencent.tim. prefix is used at runtime, never a bare com.tencent., for the same
            reason QQ's is not: this device also has qqmusic, qqlive, tmgp.* and others installed.
        -->
        <item>com.tencent.tim</item>

        <!--
            v9.5.0: Xiaohongshu (小红书) is the SIXTH host.

            ============================== WHY THIS ENTRY IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS MOST ==============================

            This array IS the LSPosed scope picker's list. Omitting a host here means the user cannot tick
            it at all, so every hook in this module is unreachable for that app, no matter how correctly
            it was written. That is exactly what happened when this host was first added, and the user
            reported it plainly:

                「怎么推荐作用域推荐应用没有小红书了」

            Adding the host to `HostApp` is therefore only HALF the work. A new host touches FIVE places,
            and the first three produce a compile error or a failing test if forgotten while these last
            two fail SILENTLY in the UI:

              1. `HostApp`: the enum constant, its packages and `of()`; a missed `when` branch will not
                 compile, which is the safety net.
              2. `RealHostsListEveryHostAndExcludeUnknown`: an exact-list test that fails until updated.
              3. `WeChatHooks`: the host's own hook arm; a missed `when` branch will not compile.
              4. THIS ARRAY: the scope picker's contents. No error, no test failure: the app simply
                 cannot be selected.
              5. The manifest `queries` block: likewise no error, because `getPackageInfo` throws on
                 Android 11+ and the UI reports 「未安装」 for an app that is installed and running.

            com.xingin.xhs is the ANDROID package. The APK's internal classes live under `com.xingin.*`
            (`com.xingin.im.*`, `com.xingin.chatbase.bean.*`), but that namespace is NOT a package and
            must not appear here. Runtime resolution additionally accepts a `com.xingin.xhs.` suffix for
            a third-party clone, via the prefix rule in HostApp.of.

            Kept in sync with HostApp.XIAOHONGSHU_PACKAGES, which decides the host at runtime.
        -->
        <item>com.xingin.xhs</item>
    </string-array>
</resources>
